.TH STATFS 2
.SH NAME
statfs, fstatfs \- free space of a mounted file system
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.ft B
#include <sys/statfs.h>

int statfs(const char *\fIpath\fP, struct statfs *\fIbuf\fP)
int fstatfs(int \fIfd\fP, struct statfs *\fIbuf\fP)
.ft R
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Statfs
and
.B fstatfs
fill in
.I buf
for the mounted file system that holds
.I path
or the open file
.IR fd :
.PP
.nf
.ta +4n +10n
	long	f_bsize;	/* block size: 1024 */
	long	f_blocks;	/* data zones */
	long	f_bfree;	/* free zones */
	long	f_bavail;	/* free zones for ordinary users */
	long	f_files;	/* inodes */
	long	f_ffree;	/* free inodes */
	dev_t	f_dev;	/* the device */
	short	f_namemax;	/* longest name: 14 (V1/V2) or 60 (V3) */
	short	f_version;	/* 1, 2 or 3 */
	short	f_flag;	/* ST_RDONLY, ST_ESTIMATE */
.fi
.PP
The answer comes from counters the file system server keeps up to date
when it allocates and frees zones and inodes; no bit map is read, so it is
quick on any size.  At mount the counters start from a hint stored in the
super block when the file system was last marked clean, and
.B ST_ESTIMATE
is set until
.BR update (8)
has recounted the bit maps, a few blocks at a time while the system is
idle.  Linux does not know the hint, so after Linux wrote to the file system
the estimate can be off until the recount ends.
.PP
.BR fpathconf (3)
and
.BR pathconf (3)
answer
.B _PC_NAME_MAX
from
.BR f_namemax .
.SH "RETURN VALUE"
0, or \-1 with
.I errno
set.
.SH ERRORS
.TP 15
[EBADF]
.I fd
is not an open file.
.TP 15
[EINVAL]
The file is not on a mounted file system (a pipe).
.TP 15
[ENOENT]
.I path
does not exist.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR df (1),
.BR stat (2),
.BR mount (2).
